In any case, and just for clarification and understanding, what are the concrete rules or protocol that require requests to be made “by a Chief or Director” and to what extent the fact that it is the Inspector of the Judiciary Police who requests the information pinches the “data protection culture” or the “integrity of the requested information”?

This is because:

The investigation within the aforementioned NUIPC is delegated, legally and judicially, to the Judiciary Police;

The Judiciary Police, with the exception of acts reserved for the Public Prosecutor's Office and the Investigating Judges, have the legitimacy to carry out any measures considered pertinent during a criminal investigation, including access and request for information (non-classified) to entities that hold it;

The Inspector of the Judiciary Police is a criminal police body. It is a competent authority for the processing of personal data within the scope of the activity he carries out in the prevention, investigation, detection or repression of criminal offences, being his competence, as a worker in the investigation career. crime, carry out the mission and attributions of the PJ, within the scope of prevention, detection and criminal investigation (art. 39 of DL n.º 138/2019, of 13 September).

Under art. 1st paragraph c) of the Code of Criminal Procedure means «Criminal police bodies» all entities and police agents who are responsible for carrying out any acts ordered by a judicial authority or determined by this Code;

138/2019, of September 13 (PROFESSIONAL STATUTE OF JUDICIARY POLICE PERSONNEL), which refers to the functional content of the functions of Inspector of the Judiciary Police, provides, among others, for the instruction of cases -crime and carrying out acts of prevention and criminal investigation with technical autonomy in the planning and execution of the corresponding procedural steps.

The request for information was based on provisions of the Portuguese legal system that constitute a necessary and proportionate measure, in a democratic society, to safeguard the objectives referred to in article 23, paragraph 1 of the RGPD, which imposes limitations to ensure, namely:

d) The prevention, investigation, detection or repression of criminal offences, or the execution of criminal sanctions, including safeguarding and preventing threats to public security;

f) The defence of judicial independence and judicial processes;

The data that is intended to be collected is for specific, explicit and legitimate purposes, limited to the minimum necessary about the purpose for which they are processed and will be subject to lawful processing, under the terms of Law n.º 59/2019 of 8th of August, which approves the rules relating to the processing of personal data to prevent, detect, investigating or repressing criminal offences or the execution of criminal sanctions, transposing Directive (EU) 2016/680 of the European Parliament and of the Council, of 27 April 2016;

So much so that, when they are used for criminal investigation, under the terms of Law No. 59/2019 of August 8, they are considered competent authorities for their treatment, under art. 3rd paragraph 1 subparagraph i) of that same public authority responsible for the prevention, investigation, detection or repression of criminal offences or the execution of criminal sanctions, including the safeguarding and prevention of threats to public security, or any other body or entity who exercises, under the terms of the law, public authority and public powers for the purposes as mentioned above;

And, under paragraph 3 of the same article 3, for paragraph i) of paragraph 1, the competent authorities are the security forces and services, the criminal police bodies (cf. art. 1 c) of the CPP), the judicial authorities and the prison and social reintegration services, within the scope of their attributions of prevention, detection, investigation or repression of criminal offences or execution of criminal sanctions, under the terms provided for in the respective statutes and laws internal security, organization of criminal investigation and criminal proceedings.

Best regards,

